      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2010 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "This study reports that peripheral administration of Nerve Growth Factor antibodies (ANA) affects behavior in aged female CD-1 mice. ANA increased the propensity of mice to stay and perform behaviors in the anxiogenic open arms of the maze, lowered pain sensitivity and reduced behavioral flexibility in a Morris water maze task, also reducing ChAT immunoreactivity in the basal forebrain. These findings support the hypothesis that topical eye application can represent an alternative route for delivering biologically active compounds into the brain allowing studying the role of NGF on brain cell function."
      },
